~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
[EXPLAIN] Reading the interval
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Every predicted potency is reported as pIC50 +/- 0.99. The interval comes from the absolute
  errors this same model made on the 129 compounds held out in step 04, and covers the true value
  of a new compound 90% of the time provided that compound resembles the held-out ones. For a drug
  outside the applicability domain the coverage is not guaranteed, and the interval should be read
  as a lower bound on the         uncertainty, not as its measure.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

[5/5] PERFORMANCE REPORTS AND EXPORT

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
[EXPLAIN] Report A: sanity check against known drugs
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Pyrimethamine and trimethoprim are classic DHFR inhibitors with known experimental pIC50. If the
  model predicts them         close to the lab value it is behaving sensibly. Status: absolute
  error < 0.5 = EXCELLENT, < 1.0 = ACCEPTABLE, else ALERT.         Caution: if these drugs (or
  close analogues) are in the training set, a good match is partly memorisation and not a
  true test of generalisation.
